// The grid cell between rows 4k and 4k+1 is tile k's quad. Every other cell
// has two coincident vertices on each of its rows, so whatever triangles the
// mesh splits it into have zero area and raster nothing. Unused tiles collapse
// onto the origin the same way.
//
// WHY ONE MESH AND NOT ONE ITEM PER TILE. Two items meeting on an edge each
// carry their own matrix, and the shared edge is computed twice in float32 --
// at a fractional zoom it can land either side of a pixel centre, and that
// pixel is then drawn twice (darker) or not at all (a hole). Here every vertex
// goes through ONE matrix and neighbouring tiles read the SAME uniform numbers
// for their shared edge, so the rasteriser's shared-edge rule hands each pixel
// to exactly one tile.
//
// Uniform arrays do not bind from QML in Qt 6.11 (measured: a `var` array on a
// `vec4 x[3]` renders black, three `vector4d`s render), hence t0..t95.
//
// THE SHELL'S EDGE. The list holds two kinds of tile, each in a half of its own: slots
// 0 .. 47 hold the SHADOW's tiles (the first `shadowTiles` of them live), slots 48 .. 95
// the shape's (the first `tileCount`). A fixed split, so that a shadow tile that comes or
// goes moves no other tile's numbers (a uniform write costs a trip through the property
// system). A shadow tile is drawn FIRST (the mesh's
// triangles are rasterised in order, so the shape lands on top of it) and is the
// same field evaluated at the same point, read a second way (a halo, not a drop shadow:
// no offset moves vPos; shadowParam.xy
// is unused). The tile says what it is by the SIGN of vTile
// (-1 - k for a shadow tile); the ink (colours, reach, border width) the fragment
// shader reads from the uniform block itself, at its very end -- as varyings they
// were registers held through the whole evaluation (gooey_field_ubo.py).
// =============================================================================

